  "summary": "Synthesized both previous contributions into a two-tier approach that resolves the core tension between simplicity and diagnostic power. Tier 1 (implement now): Replace `_get_latest_feedback()` with `_get_accumulated_feedback()` that returns a structured summary of ALL prior review feedback for the current stage, not just the latest — this is a ~30-line change to phases.py that captures 80% of the value with zero new infrastructure. The accumulated feedback should include attempt number, verdict, and key feedback points for each prior attempt, presented as a numbered history so the agent can see patterns itself. Tier 2 (implement only if Tier 1 proves insufficient): Rather than a full diagnostic meta-agent as originally proposed, add a lightweight inline diagnostic *prompt section* — not a separate agent or phase — that activates on attempt N-1 (the penultimate attempt before exhausting retries). This is just an additional prompt block injected into the existing `run_fix_feedback()` or `run_planning()` call that says 'This is your final attempt. Here is the full history of prior failures: [accumulated feedback]. Identify why previous attempts failed and take a fundamentally different approach if needed.' This avoids all the complexity concerns from the second critique (no new phase type, no meta-retry problem, no model selection debate, no token efficiency tension) while still providing the 'step back and analyze' behavior the original proposal wanted. The key insight both previous contributors missed: the agent doing the work is already the best-positioned entity to diagnose its own failures — it just needs the full failure history and an explicit instruction to analyze patterns rather than blindly address only the latest feedback. A separate diagnostic agent adds indirection without adding intelligence. The only case where escalation logic is genuinely needed is when max retries are exhausted, and the system already handles this by moving to 'final-human-approval' — the improvement there is simply to include the accumulated feedback history in that transition so the human reviewer has full context."
